  "worked_solution": "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=1=f(-1).\\,\u2713\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(3).\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at $x=-1.$",
